markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h1>What Is Accessibility, and Why AI Makes It Urgent</h1>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px;">
<p>📖 <b>Accessibility</b> is the practice of designing products, websites, and spaces so that people with a wide range of abilities, disabilities, and needs can use them fully and independently.</p>
</div>

<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/37181a0cc0dcabbcc8967805c2905dd0-image-1786029493965.png" alt="A simplified webpage mockup illustrating common accessibility issues: a broken image icon labeled image123.jpg with no caption, low-contrast gray text and headings that are hard to read, and two unlabeled form fields for entering an email, next to an unlabeled arrow button." style="width: 35%; max-width: 220px; border-radius: 10px;">
  <p style="flex: 1;">Nearly 95% of websites have some kind of accessibility issue. The most common ones show up constantly in AI-generated layouts too: low contrast text, missing alternative text, missing form input labels, and empty links or buttons.</p>
</div>

<h2>AI and Patterns of Exclusion</h2>

You've already seen how AI generates pages: it learns patterns from a massive collection of real websites, then predicts new output based on what it's seen most often. That's exactly why accessibility gaps show up so reliably in AI-generated code. 

***If low contrast text or unlabeled buttons appear across huge amounts of the web AI trained on, AI reproduces that pattern right along with the useful ones.***

<p>Recognizing patterns of exclusion is the first step to correcting them. That's true of AI the same way it's true of a human designer: neither catches an accessibility gap automatically, it takes someone deciding to look for it.</p>

---
